CS2102 IITPSx Processor Project - Claims

Source files are written in C++:
- assembler/asm.cpp is the two-pass assembler source.
- emulator/emu.cpp is the emulator source.
- make builds both programs with the supplied Makefile.

Assembler:
- One shared routine processes source in both passes.
- An instruction table defines mnemonics, opcodes, and operand kinds.
- Supports labels, numeric operands, PC-relative branches, data, and SET.
- Reports errors including duplicate/invalid labels, undefined labels,
  unknown mnemonics, and invalid or missing operands.
- Produces binary object files and human-readable listings.
- Includes mul and div opcodes.

Emulator:
- Loads and executes binary object files.
- Provides memory dumps, tracing, and disassembly.
- Implements mul and signed div, including division-by-zero detection.
- Detects invalid PC and memory access, unknown opcodes, and instruction-limit
  exhaustion.

Tests in Tests/:
- test01: valid syntax, labels, branches, data, and an infinite-loop example.
- test02: malformed input and assembler diagnostics.
- test03: SET directive and execution.
- test04: recursive triangle-number program.
- test05: sum of integers 1 through 10.
- test06: unused-label warnings and an infinite-loop example.
- test07: additional malformed-input and SET/DATA diagnostics.
- test08: n*(n+1)/2 using mul and div.
- test09: emulator division-by-zero error handling.
- test10: bubble sort of five values.
- Successful assemblies include matching .o and .lst files. Each test has a
  .log recording the assembler result and, when assembled, emulator result.
- Invalid-input tests have an explanatory .lst file noting that the assembler
  correctly rejected the source and did not generate a listing.

The included algorithm tests are the triangle-number, summation, arithmetic
formula, and bubble-sort programs. The logs record their observed outcomes.
